      "source": [
        "### What we noticed (resumes)\n",
        "1. **Most categories are balanced, but three are small.** 21 categories have 96 to 120 resumes. Agriculture has 63, Automobile 36 and BPO 22 (5.45 times fewer than the biggest). So we keep the same share of each category in the training and test sets, give small categories more weight, and use a score (macro-F1) that counts every category the same.\n",
        "2. **Almost every resume starts with a job title.** One was found in 2,439 of 2,481 resumes (98.3%). In 65% of resumes a word from the category name is in the title, and in 9 categories (HR, Sales, Accountant, Finance, Engineering, Business-Development, Designer, Consultant, Teacher) it is 97% or more. So the title can give the answer away, and we train with and without it.\n",
        "3. **Resume length varies.** The middle resume has 741 words and half have between 635 and 913. The shortest has 94 and the longest 5,159, and 148 are above 1,330 words. Length is similar across categories, so it will not tell them apart.\n",
        "4. **Categories have their own words, but some are shared.** Accountant has \"ledger\" and \"tax\". \"management\" is in the top 10 of 15 categories, and \"customer\" and \"sales\" in 12. The small categories (BPO, Automobile) have mostly general words, so we expect the lowest scores there.\n",
        "5. **Some categories are close.** Accountant and Finance, Advocate and Healthcare, and Apparel and Sales all have a similarity of 0.84. Arts and Teacher have 0.80. The model may mix them up, and we will check this in the confusion matrix.\n",
        "6. **These are experienced professionals, not students.** 795 of 2,439 titles (33%) sound senior and only 190 (8%) sound junior. Of the 867 resumes that state years of experience, the middle one claims 10 years. A model trained on them may not understand a student CV, and this is a limitation.\n",
        "7. **Some labels may be wrong.** One resume labelled CHEF (ID 15180322) is about a nursing student with a CNA license. This limits how accurate any model can be."
      ]
