      "source": [
        "### What we noticed (ESCO skills)\n",
        "1. **Skill names are short phrases.** The average name has 3.6 words and 78% have 4 words or fewer. But only 68% of all lookup spellings do, so about a third are longer than our 4-word search and can never be found.\n",
        "2. **Synonyms do the work.** Skills have 6.2 synonyms on average (up to 78 for \"graphics editor software\"), and only 18 have none. Hidden labels are empty for 13,786 of 13,939 skills. We kept 99,874 spellings.\n",
        "3. **Most skills are sector-specific.** 6,655 are sector-specific, 3,783 cross-sector and 3,044 occupation-specific, and only 452 are transversal (about 3%). Soft skills are rare.\n",
        "4. **ESCO misses some everyday words.** \"teamwork\" and \"docker\" are not in the lookup, and \"react\" maps to \"JavaScript Framework\". This matters for IT and soft-skill gaps.\n",
        "5. **Exact matching finds many skills, but many are generic.** The middle resume has 17 skills found and none of the 2,481 has zero. The middle posting has 10, and 12 of 1,000 have none. But the most common skills in resumes are \"communication\" (1,476), \"plan\" (1,420) and \"lead others\" (1,417), which come from everyday words.\n",
        "6. **Short spellings caused false matches, so we removed them.** Spellings of 1 or 2 letters (like \"it\", \"pr\", \"pm\", \"ar\") match ordinary words, so only c# is left. Longer everyday words still match, such as \"stand\" and \"commitment\".\n",
        "7. **ESCO finds more skills in technical resumes.** Information Technology resumes have about 25 skills found on average and Engineering about 24, against about 13 for Teacher and 14 for Chef. ESCO's wording fits technical jobs better, so the gap diagnosis will be more reliable for them."
      ]
