        "### Set up the models\n",
        "\n",
        "A model cannot read words, so each resume is first turned into numbers. We give every word a score (TF-IDF, as in the EDA), and the model learns from these scores. The scores are learned from the training resumes only, also inside every practice round below. We compare three models: logistic regression (our main model), a linear SVM (a similar model) and Naive Bayes (a simple, fast model). We add a baseline that always guesses the most common category, because any real model must beat it."
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "execution_count": 214,
      "metadata": {
        "id": "1ZBTe66BXsC1"
      },
      "outputs": [],
      "source": [
        "# TF-IDF and the model in one pipeline, so TF-IDF is learned from the training part only\n",
        "def make_pipeline(model):\n",
        "    tfidf = TfidfVectorizer(stop_words='english', ngram_range=(1, 2), min_df=5,\n",
        "                            max_df=0.8, sublinear_tf=True, max_features=20000)\n",
        "    return Pipeline([('tfidf', tfidf), ('model', clone(model))])\n",
        "\n",
        "# 5 rounds of cross-validation, each with the same share of every category\n",
        "cv = StratifiedKFold(n_splits=5, shuffle=True, random_state=42)\n",
        "\n",
        "BASELINE = 'Baseline (most common category)'\n",
        "models = {\n",
        "    'Logistic regression': LogisticRegression(max_iter=2000, class_weight='balanced'),\n",
        "    'Linear SVM': LinearSVC(class_weight='balanced'),\n",
        "    'Naive Bayes': ComplementNB(),\n",
        "    BASELINE: DummyClassifier(strategy='most_frequent'),\n",
        "}"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {
        "id": "etBRRyC_Xtlo"
      },
      "source": [
        "### Choose the setting C\n",
        "\n",
        "Logistic regression has one setting, C. If C is too high, the model copies the training resumes too closely, and if it is too low, it learns too little. We try three values (1, 10 and 100) with practice rounds (cross-validation) on the training resumes: we split them into 5 parts, train on 4, check on the 5th, and repeat this 5 times. We score each value with macro-F1, a score from 0 to 1 that counts every category the same, so small categories matter as much as big ones. The test resumes are not used here. The best value was C = 100 (score 0.619). C = 10 is almost as good (0.613), the difference is smaller than the usual change between rounds (0.02), and 100 is the highest value we tried, so a higher value might do a bit better. Only logistic regression was tuned, the other models use their normal settings."
