        "### What we noticed (modelling)\n",
        "\n",
        "1. **The baseline is low.** Always guessing the most common category is right for about 5% of the test resumes (macro-F1 0.004). Every real model does better.\n",
        "2. **The models are close.** Linear SVM has the best score in the practice rounds (0.639) and logistic regression, our main model, has 0.619. The gap is small, while Naive Bayes is clearly lower (0.499). On the test resumes, logistic regression with the title is right for 69% of them.\n",
        "3. **The title helps a little, and works alone.** Without the title, logistic regression drops from 0.619 to 0.591 (right for 66% of the test resumes instead of 69%). The title alone scores 0.646, higher than the whole resume, so the title is a strong clue by itself. The model on the whole resume probably uses it less because the title is a few words among about 800.\n",
        "4. **The results are steady.** The practice-round scores and the test scores are within 0.03 of each other.\n",
        "5. **Some categories are hard.** The four biggest mix-ups (Finance with Accountant, Advocate with Healthcare, Arts with Teacher, Apparel with Sales) are the pairs the EDA found to be most alike (0.80 to 0.84). The lowest scores are BPO (4 test resumes), Consultant and Apparel. The last two have 23 and 19 test resumes, and their resumes are spread over neighbouring categories.\n",
