      "source": [
        "### What we noticed (skills)\n",
        "\n",
        "1. **Most skills found are right.** In the hand check, 20 of 30 skills were really in the text (0.67). The wrong ones were words used with another meaning (\"call center\", \"reliability\" of servers, \"integrity\" of documents, \"cardiac history\") or names of a field (\"banking\", \"financial planning\").\n",
        "2. **Matched postings share more skills.** The resume shows 34% of the skills in its closest postings, against 18% for random postings. This is partly expected, because the closest postings are found from the same words, so it does not prove the skill finding is right on its own.\n",
        "3. **Limits.** We did not count the skills we missed, and ESCO misses words like \"teamwork\" and \"docker\". Common skills like \"communication\" are right but generic. The missing-skills list will include some general skills."
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {
        "id": "yB8B14oNY3Gf"
      },
      "source": [
        "### What we noticed (all together)\n",
        "\n",
        "1. **Adding skills does not help.** Text and skills together find the right field 51% of the time, against 59% for text only and 14% for skills only (8% by chance). So we rank the postings with the text and use the skills only for the missing-skills list. Skills alone are still better than chance.\n",
        "2. **The whole app works end to end.** For a resume it returns matching postings and a list of missing skills. The lists are mixed: some skills are useful (SQL, WordPress, employment law, recruit employees) and some come from everyday words (LESS, adjust to physical demands, morality).\n",
        "3. **The classifier supports the matching.** The classifier is not part of the app, but it shows that the text of a resume carries its field: it is right for 66% of the test resumes without the title.\n",
        "4. **What we could not test.** There are no real resume and job pairs, no Kenyan postings and no student resumes. The scores show the method, not how it will work for our users."
      ]
